CYCLES(1)                      P32W Unix                       CYCLES(1)

NAME
       cycles - measure a command with the emulator's clock counter

SYNOPSIS
       cycles command [arg ...]
       cycles start
       cycles stop

DESCRIPTION
       cycles is for the emulator, not the real machine: there, use
       time(1). It runs the command as time(1) does and prints its
       real, user and system time on the standard error. Around the
       command it also tells the emulator to count, by raising and
       lowering bit 7 of the I/O card's output port (/dev/port). The
       port's other bits are left at 0.

       To run several steps, use cycles sh -c 'step; step', or
       cycles start, the steps, and cycles stop; those two only
       start and stop the count.

       The count covers the whole machine, not one process:
       anything else running at the time is in the count.

THE EMULATOR
       In p32uemu -G (emulators.md) the bit starts and stops a
       64-bit count of the machine's clocks. When it falls, the
       emulator reports on the host's terminal the cycles and the
       time they take at the machine's clock, the instructions, the
       idle cycles, the disk's sectors and commands, and the bytes
       sent to the console:

       [cycles #1: 7,006 cycles, 0:00:00.0 at 3 MHz (0.002 s)
        2,002 instructions, 0.86 MIPS busy; idle 0 cycles (0.0%)
        disk 0 sectors read, 0 written, 0 commands; console 0 bytes]

       The emulator's devices take no time, so the count is the
       processor's work. On the real machine add the drive's access
       time for each disk command, and the time the console's bytes
       take at its baud rate.

EXAMPLES
       cycles make
       cycles sh -c 'make clean; make'

EXIT STATUS
       The command's exit status; 128 plus the signal number if it
       was killed by a signal; 127 if it could not be run; 2 for a
       usage error or if cycles could not fork. cycles start and
       stop exit 1 if /dev/port could not be opened.

SEE ALSO
       time(1), times(2)
